## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about worst-case guarantees and practical trade-offs between quicksort and mergesort, including depth control, partition balance, and memory usage in a real-world data processing pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- Hybrid sorting (introspective sort)
- Worst-case guarantees, space complexity
- Handling duplicates, stability considerations
- Practical performance on sorted/partially sorted data

## Code Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>

// Simplified hybrid sort: quicksort with depth limit, switch to mergesort when needed
int partition(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r) {
  long long pivot = a[(l + r) / 2];
  int i = l, j = r;
  while (i <= j) {
    while (a[i] < pivot) ++i;
    while (a[j] > pivot) --j;
    if (i <= j) std::swap(a[i++], a[j--]);
  }
  return i;
}

void mergeSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, std::vector<long long>& tmp) {
  if (r - l <= 1) return;
  int m = (l + r) / 2;
  mergeSort(a, l, m, tmp);
  mergeSort(a, m, r, tmp);
  int i = l, j = m, k = 0;
  while (i < m || j < r) {
    if (j >= r || (i < m && a[i] <= a[j])) tmp[k++] = a[i++];
    else tmp[k++] = a[j++];
  }
  for (int t = 0; t < k; ++t) a[l + t] = tmp[t];
}

void hybridSort(std::vector<long long>& a, int l, int r, int depth, int depthLimit) {
  if (r - l <= 16) {
    std::sort(a.begin() + l, a.begin() + r + 1);
    return;
  }
  if (depth >= depthLimit) {
    std::vector<long long> tmp(r - l + 1);
    mergeSort(a, l, r + 1, tmp);
    return;
  }
  int p = partition(a, l, r);
  hybridSort(a, l, p - 1, depth + 1, depthLimit);
  hybridSort(a, p, r, depth + 1, depthLimit);
}

// Entry: call with depth=0 and depthLimit=2*log2(n)
```

